package philosophers

// RepublicNotes are checked facts about the structure of the Republic, in our own words, written from a reading of the text (Shorey's
// translation, Perseus; DIALECTIC.md 15 and TECHNIQUES_PLATO.md). They are given to the second check on a draft (internal/review), never to
// the philosopher: he stays free to speak, and the check has something firmer than the model's memory to hold a draft against. Section
// numbers are Stephanus numbers. Where readers disagree, the notes say so and take no side.
const RepublicNotes = `The Republic (use these only if the conversation concerns it).
Status of the city, said in the work itself: the city is built "in words" as a pattern of a good city, to see justice (5.472b-e); action reaches exact truth less than speech, so it need not be realised exactly as spoken (5.473a); it is called possible but hard, and only if philosophers rule or rulers philosophise (5.473b-e); Socrates fears the proposals will be taken as a wish-thought and puts off the question of possibility (5.450c-d, 458a-b); Glaucon says the city is nowhere on earth, and Socrates adds that perhaps a pattern is laid up in heaven for whoever wishes to look and constitute himself by it, whether or not it exists or ever will (9.592a-b). The end of the discussion turns to the constitution inside the person (9.589-592).
What the proposals are: the guardians' breeding is arranged by the rulers in secret, with contrived lots so that the inferior blame chance, the offspring of the inferior and the defective are disposed of or hidden, and this is called falsehood and deception used as a medicine (5.459-461). Socrates himself calls the story of the metals a lie, a Phoenician tale (3.414b-e).
Where the work limits or reverses them: the metals story lets children move between classes by the nature of their souls, so class is not simply by birth (3.415b-c; also 5.468a); in Book 8 the Muses, speaking in a mock-serious tragic style (8.545e), say that the wise rulers will miss the right times for births, and the best city decays (8.546a-d); the first stage of decay reverses the earlier claim that the guardians' people are supporters and not slaves (8.547b-c; compare 5.463a-b); the community of wives and children applies to the guardians only.
Features some readers take as irony, parody or an unreliable construction, and others take at face value (no side is taken here): Socrates being forced into the topic and his repeated shrinking from it ("waves", 5.449-451, 457, 472-473); the "wish-thought" and daydreamer language; the dog and bird breeding analogy (5.451-459); the "ingenious lots" (5.460a); the secrecy and the lie as medicine (5.459-461); the Muses and the number (8.545-547); the final lines (9.592). Whether the city is meant ironically is disputed. A claim that nothing in the text points that way is as unsupported as a claim that it is all irony: the features are there and readers differ.`
